<BaseLayout title="Support" description="How to get help with qyl and the hosted qyl MCP service.">
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="reading-shell">
      <span class="eyebrow">Support · qyl</span>
      <h1 class="page-title">Get help with qyl.</h1>
      <p class="lede">qyl lets your agent investigate traces, logs, recorded metrics and sessions from the collector project your account is authorized to access. Its telemetry tools read data. Its write tools change annotations, saved queries and failure status in qyl; no tool changes your telemetry or your systems.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="editorial-section editorial-section--raised">
    <article class="reading-shell docs-content">
      <h2>Contact</h2>
      <p>Write to <a href="mailto:anfh22@outlook.com">anfh22@outlook.com</a>. This address is read by the operator, Alexander Nachtmann (ancplua). Use it for technical issues, questions about the hosted MCP service at <code>https://mcp.qyl.at/mcp</code>, account access, privacy requests and deletion requests.</p>

      <h2>Access to the hosted endpoint</h2>
      <p>The hosted MCP service serves accounts the operator has set up: each account is mapped to one Collector project, and each host registers itself with qyl's Auth0 tenant when it signs in, so no host needs a client ID. To get access, write to the address above. The local Collector and the local stdio server need no account; <a href="/docs/getting-started/">getting started</a> covers them.</p>

      <h2>What to include in a technical report</h2>
      <p>Name the client and its version (for example ChatGPT, Claude, Claude Code, Codex or MCP Inspector), the tool or operation you tried, the approximate time with timezone, and what you saw. A small, sanitized reproduction helps most.</p>
      <p>Do not send passwords, access tokens, signing keys, private telemetry or other people's data. If a trace or log record is part of the problem, send its identifier rather than its content.</p>

      <h2>Account, privacy and deletion requests</h2>
      <p>Requests about your sign-in account, about data the service holds for you, or about deletion go to the same address. The operator may ask you to confirm that you control the account before changing access or removing account-related records. Describe the request; never include your credentials.</p>

      <h2>Annotations, saved queries and failure status</h2>
      <p>Creating and updating annotations and saved queries and setting failure status needs the <code>qyl:write</code> permission; deleting an annotation or a saved query needs <code>qyl:delete</code>. A call without the permission it needs is refused with an <code>insufficient_scope</code> challenge such as “annotate needs the qyl:write scope”. Reconnect qyl in your client and allow that permission; retrying the call alone does not help. If your sign-in cannot grant it, write to the address above. An update or deletion that fails with <code>precondition_failed</code> means the object changed after you read it: read it again and repeat the change.</p>

      <h2>Telemetry and project records</h2>
      <p>Failure status controls whether triage shows an ignored failure or reports a resolved failure that recurs. It does not change the underlying telemetry. <code>delete_record</code> removes only an annotation or a saved query, not telemetry or copies a connected client already received.</p>

      <h2>Source and documentation</h2>
      <p>The Collector, the MCP server and the qyl plugin are open source at <a href="https://github.com/ANcpLua/qyl">ANcpLua/qyl</a>; this site is at <a href="https://github.com/ANcpLua/qyl.at">ANcpLua/qyl.at</a>. <a href="/docs/mcp/">Connect an agent</a> documents the tools, scopes and skills.</p>
    </article>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
